# Abstract

Bitcoin's security model depends on the ability of independently operated full nodes to validate blocks and transactions according to the network's consensus rules. Bitcoin Core's full-validation model allows users to independently verify the blockchain rather than relying solely on third parties for the correctness of received data.

The security of a decentralised network, however, is not determined exclusively by protocol correctness. The practical operation of the infrastructure that implements and supports the network also matters.

Bitcoin full nodes operate across a diverse range of environments, including dedicated servers, cloud infrastructure, personal computers, single-board computers and resource-constrained deployments. These environments are exposed to operational conditions such as power interruptions, network outages, intermittent connectivity, storage pressure, hardware limitations and software failures.

This paper presents an initial systematic review and technology landscape analysis undertaken as part of the BNSAT-R research programme of UBRO CLASSIFIEDS LIMITED in Uganda.

The objective is not to claim that Bitcoin resilience is an unexplored field. Recent research has examined Bitcoin's resilience to physical Internet infrastructure disruptions, while other work has investigated resource consumption and operational characteristics during initial block download. Existing projects such as myNode, RaspiBlitz and Umbrel also provide node management, monitoring and deployment capabilities.

Instead, this review investigates whether there remains a research opportunity at the intersection of:

1. Individual full-node operational resilience;
2. Controlled infrastructure disruptions;
3. Resource-constrained environments;
4. Reproducible resilience measurement;
5. Recovery and synchronisation behaviour.

The preliminary findings suggest that the broad concept of Bitcoin network resilience is already being studied, and several practical node-management solutions already exist. Consequently, a new project should not simply duplicate node-management or monitoring functionality.

A potentially valuable research direction may instead be the development and validation of a reproducible experimental methodology for measuring the operational resilience and recovery behaviour of individual Bitcoin full-node deployments under controlled disruptions.

This paper therefore recommends that UBRO proceed to a structured research-validation and pilot-design phase before committing to development of a new software platform.

