# 7. Existing Technology Landscape

## 7.1 myNode

myNode is designed to make running a Bitcoin and Lightning node easier and more accessible.

Its project documentation describes goals including:

* Ease of operation;
* Uptime;
* Reliability;
* Management;
* Support.

myNode therefore demonstrates that operational reliability and node management are already recognised practical concerns.

This reduces the justification for creating another general-purpose node-management platform.

A BNSAT-R research project should instead investigate whether existing platforms provide sufficient tools for **standardised resilience experimentation and measurement**.

---

## 7.2 RaspiBlitz

RaspiBlitz provides a DIY Bitcoin full-node and Lightning environment, including monitoring and management capabilities.

Its documentation describes the project as an educational and decentralised approach to operating a node, with additional services accessible through its WebUI.

RaspiBlitz demonstrates that node deployment and monitoring can be performed in relatively accessible hardware environments.

This may be relevant to BNSAT-R research into resource-constrained deployments.

However, the existence of RaspiBlitz also reinforces the need to distinguish between:

* Deploying a node;
* Managing a node;
* Monitoring a node;
* Experimentally measuring node resilience.

These are not necessarily the same research problem.

---

## 7.3 Umbrel

Umbrel provides an accessible environment for operating Bitcoin nodes and related applications.

Its Bitcoin application supports Bitcoin Core and provides configuration options relating to:

* Bitcoin Core versions;
* Network connectivity;
* Tor;
* I2P;
* Upload limits;
* Mempool size;
* Performance.

Umbrel therefore provides significant operational controls.

The existence of these capabilities means that BNSAT-R should carefully assess whether any proposed monitoring or management functionality would duplicate existing work.

