# 20. Limitations

This preliminary review has several limitations.

First, it is not yet a completed systematic literature review.

Second, the existence of potentially similar research cannot be ruled out.

Third, the research has not yet undergone extensive expert review by established Bitcoin Core developers or security researchers.

Fourth, the proposed metrics have not yet been experimentally validated.

Fifth, the relationship between individual node resilience and overall Bitcoin network security requires careful treatment.

A failure of an individual node does not necessarily constitute a security failure of the Bitcoin network.

The proposed research must therefore avoid overstating the security implications of operational resilience.

---

# 21. Conclusion

The initial research undertaken by the BNSAT-R programme indicates that Bitcoin resilience is a broad and active field of research.

Recent work has investigated resilience at the physical infrastructure and network levels.

Existing projects provide substantial capabilities for Bitcoin node deployment, management and monitoring.

Existing research has also examined Bitcoin node resource behaviour.

These findings suggest that UBRO should not attempt to create another general-purpose Bitcoin node management platform.

Instead, a potentially more valuable contribution may lie in investigating the operational resilience of individual Bitcoin full-node deployments through controlled, reproducible experiments.

The central research hypothesis is that a systematic methodology for measuring node recovery behaviour under controlled disruptions and resource constraints may provide useful information that complements existing network-level resilience research and node-management tooling.

This hypothesis requires further validation.

The recommended next step is therefore a structured eight-week research programme combining:

* Systematic literature review;
* Technology landscape analysis;
* Existing methodology review;
* Expert technical feedback;
* Research-gap validation;
* Experimental design.

Only after this process should UBRO decide whether to build BNRB, extend an existing project, collaborate with established researchers or pivot to another research question.

The ultimate objective is not to build a new tool for its own sake.

The objective is to identify a meaningful security or resilience problem and contribute evidence, research and open-source work that the broader Bitcoin ecosystem can independently evaluate and use.

